Describe a skill that you learned from older people
- what the skill is
- who you learned it from
- how you learned it
- how you feel about it

One skill I learned from an older person is gardening, and I learned it from an elderly man who lived in my neighborhood back in my hometown. He was well known for having one of the most beautiful gardens in the area, so many people admired him for that.
I started learning from him a few years ago during my summer holiday. At first, I was just curious and often stopped by to watch him take care of his plants. He was very friendly and patient, so he gradually taught me some basic gardening skills, like planting seeds, watering plants properly, pruning branches, and using natural fertilizer. What impressed me the most was that he could tell whether a plant was healthy just by looking at its leaves.
I really enjoyed spending time with him because he not only taught me practical skills but also shared life lessons. He often told me that gardening requires patience and consistency because plants need time to grow. I think that idea can also be applied to life in general.
I enjoy gardening because it helps me relax and reduces stress. Whenever I spend time taking care of plants, I feel much calmer and closer to nature. Besides that, it’s really rewarding to see flowers bloom or vegetables grow from something you planted yourself.
Even now, I still remember the things he taught me, and I feel grateful that I had the chance to learn such a meaningful skill from him.
